<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Shikai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuan Shikai was a Chinese general who built the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a> under the Qing. In 1912 he arranged the abdication of the last emperor and became President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, then dissolved parliament and ruled as a dictator. His attempt to make himself emperor in 1915–1916 provoked revolt, and he died in June 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai.jpg" alt="Yuan Shikai" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by Rio V. De Sieux [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanShikaiPresidente1915.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuan Shikai was born on 16 September 1859 in Zhangying village, in Xiangcheng county, to an affluent Han family, and was the fourth of six sons. He received a traditional Confucian education, failed the imperial examinations twice, in 1876 and 1879, and entered public life through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-army">Huai Army</a>, in which many of his relatives served. In 1882 he went to Joseon (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>) with troops sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> to restore order, and he distinguished himself on Wu Changqing's staff. He later commanded the Chinese forces there, trained the Korean royal guard, and after he thwarted the Kapsin Coup in 1885 was made imperial resident and supreme adviser to the Korean government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1894, he was given the task of training the New Army the next year, which formed the basis of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a>. He took an active part in the late Qing reforms, including the abolition of the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>' reformers accused him of betrayal for tipping off Ronglu about their plans, which brought him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>'s favour. Appointed Viceroy of Zhili in 1902, he built the Beiyang Army into the best trained force in China. After Cixi's death the Prince Regent Zaifeng dismissed him.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a> of 10 October 1911 the Qing court recalled him, and he became prime minister on 1 November. His army fought briefly against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s revolutionaries before negotiations began. Under the deal Sun would step aside in Yuan's favour if Yuan secured the abdication of the Qing court, and Yuan became provisional president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1913 Yuan's alleged part in the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-jiaoren">Song Jiaoren</a> and the reorganisation loan affair provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s Second Revolution, which was crushed. He outlawed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> and replaced the provisional constitution. In December 1915 he proclaimed himself Hongxian Emperor, which provoked the National Protection War. Faced with widespread opposition he abdicated in March 1916 and restored the Republic, having been emperor for only 83 days. He died of kidney failure on 6 June 1916, aged 56, leaving a weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a> and a fragmented political situation that soon fell into warlordism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuan Shikai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolutionaries lacked the strength to defeat the Qing’s Beiyang Army, so Sun agreed to stand aside in favour of its commander, <b>Yuan Shikai</b>, if he secured the dynasty’s abdication.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Yuan Shikai</b> offered to restore the 13th Dalai Lama's titles; he refused, replying that he intended to exercise both temporal and ecclesiastical rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to later accounts, Tan Sitong asked <b>Yuan Shikai</b> to kill Ronglu, take control of the garrison at Tientsin and march on Beijing to arrest Cixi.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of President <b>Yuan Shikai</b> in 1916 China was dominated by warlords, and the government in Beijing could do little against foreign influence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1911 Revolution, <b>Yuan Shikai</b> ended the second revolution of the summer of 1913 and forced many intellectuals into exile, some to Tokyo and others to Shanghai.</blockquote><a href="/movement/new-culture-movement">New Culture Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan secretly pressed <b>Yuan Shikai</b>'s government for extensive rights in Shandong, Manchuria and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiangcheng City, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.44,114.9&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Xiangcheng City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7486 950 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yuan Shikai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7936 1220h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7936 1220h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.44,114.9&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
